1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:06,310
The brush finds silver, stamped with marks, and behind it the cut face of a city wall.

2
00:00:06,310 --> 00:00:07,481
Both are new.

3
00:00:12,733 --> 00:00:19,383
For roughly a thousand years after the Harappan cities emptied, nobody in India built a city or struck a coin.

4
00:00:19,383 --> 00:00:24,834
Around the sixth century BCE, both come back at once — and that is not a coincidence.

5
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:29,689
Three sources now.

6
00:00:29,689 --> 00:00:34,444
Buddhist and Jain texts, which list the states and describe their politics.

7
00:00:34,444 --> 00:00:37,822
Excavation, which gives us the walls and the pottery.

8
00:00:37,822 --> 00:00:41,813
And coins, which are new evidence and tell us about trade.

9
00:00:46,867 --> 00:00:50,438
The texts name sixteen great states, the mahajanapadas.

10
00:00:50,438 --> 00:00:57,499
Magadha, Kosala, Vatsa and Avanti are the four that matter most, because they end up swallowing the rest.

11
00:01:00,900 --> 00:01:10,750
The others include Kashi, Anga, Vajji, Malla, Chedi, Kuru, Panchala, Matsya, Surasena, Assaka, Gandhara and Kamboja.

12
00:01:10,750 --> 00:01:14,838
Learn the four that win; recognise the rest.

13
00:01:18,767 --> 00:01:23,908
And they were not all the same kind of state, which is the part worth knowing properly.

14
00:01:23,908 --> 00:01:27,947
Most were monarchies: one hereditary king, a treasury, an army.

15
00:01:32,267 --> 00:01:39,133
But some were ganasanghas, often translated as republics — governed by an assembly of heads of leading families,

16
00:01:39,133 --> 00:01:41,502
who met, debated and decided together.

17
00:01:41,502 --> 00:01:45,330
The Vajji confederacy, centred on Vaishali, is the famous one.

18
00:01:45,330 --> 00:01:49,640
The Buddha himself came from one of these, the Shakya ganasangha.

19
00:01:54,100 --> 00:01:57,037
The second urbanisation, as historians call it,

20
00:01:57,037 --> 00:02:03,277
is the return of city life to the subcontinent — this time in the Ganga plains rather than the Indus.

21
00:02:03,277 --> 00:02:06,208
Ramparts, moats, planned streets, markets.

22
00:02:10,367 --> 00:02:15,458
And a new fine pottery, Northern Black Polished Ware, so well finished it looks metallic.

23
00:02:15,458 --> 00:02:19,775
Where you find it, you are usually looking at a town with money in it.

24
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:25,913
Why now?

25
00:02:25,913 --> 00:02:28,977
Follow the chain, because examiners love it.

26
00:02:28,977 --> 00:02:32,790
Iron tools and the plough open the heavy eastern soils.

27
00:02:32,790 --> 00:02:35,650
Rice transplanting raises yields sharply.

28
00:02:35,650 --> 00:02:41,669
Surplus feeds people who are not farmers — craftsmen, traders, soldiers, officials.

29
00:02:47,567 --> 00:02:50,759
Trade needs a medium, so punch-marked coins appear.

30
00:02:50,759 --> 00:02:52,970
Trade organises itself into guilds.

31
00:02:52,970 --> 00:02:59,716
And a king who can tax a surplus can pay a standing army, which is how four states end up eating twelve.

32
00:03:05,467 --> 00:03:07,639
A naming note for this unit.

33
00:03:07,639 --> 00:03:11,758
Champa, the capital of Anga, is near modern Bhagalpur.

34
00:03:11,758 --> 00:03:13,481
Kashi is Varanasi.

35
00:03:17,233 --> 00:03:20,716
Vaishali, the seat of the Vajji confederacy, is in Bihar.

36
00:03:20,716 --> 00:03:24,499
The sources use the old names throughout, and so will we.

37
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:31,890
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

38
00:03:31,890 --> 00:03:33,174
Sixteen states.

39
00:03:33,174 --> 00:03:35,882
Two kinds: king, or assembly.

40
00:03:42,133 --> 00:03:42,835
Dig Quiz.

41
00:03:42,835 --> 00:03:44,168
Pause and name it.

42
00:03:44,168 --> 00:03:49,781
First: a state governed by an assembly of heads of families rather than a king.

43
00:03:49,781 --> 00:03:51,064
A ganasangha.

44
00:03:55,200 --> 00:04:00,406
Second: a fine, highly polished black pottery, the marker of towns in this period.

45
00:04:00,406 --> 00:04:02,587
Northern Black Polished Ware.

46
00:04:07,367 --> 00:04:12,445
Third: silver stamped with several separate marks, and no ruler's name.

47
00:04:12,445 --> 00:04:13,926
A punch-marked coin.

48
00:04:13,926 --> 00:04:19,304
In Mains, a frequent question asks what caused the second urbanisation.

49
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:30,930
Give the chain: iron, surplus, specialists, trade, coins, taxation, and the state that can afford an army.

50
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:42,593
A punch-marked coin takes its place in the gallery: the first money struck in India, and the sign that cities were back.

51
00:04:46,767 --> 00:04:51,319
Next gallery: how one of those sixteen states beat the other fifteen.

52
00:04:51,319 --> 00:04:55,325
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

